  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Conic morel (Verpa conica) is a small, bell-shaped fungus belonging to the morels and allies family, characterized by its distinctive conical or thimble-like cap. It is an early spring fruiter found in New England woodlands and is prized by foragers, though it requires proper identification to distinguish from toxic look-alikes.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Shape",
      "description": "Distinctive conical or bell-shaped cap that appears like a thimble or pointed hat, narrowing toward the apex",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Attachment",
      "description": "Cap is attached only at the top of the stem, creating a hollow, free-hanging appearance rather than running fully down the stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and Color",
      "description": "Small fruiting body (1-2 inches tall) with a pale tan to brown cap and whitish or pale stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Conic morel fruits in early spring in moist woodlands, along stream banks, and near dead or dying elm, ash, and cottonwood trees throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike true morels (Morchella species), the conic morel's cap hangs freely from the stem like a bell, which is why it's sometimes called the 'bell morel' and is less commonly foraged due to its smaller size and stricter habitat preferences.",
